
# Clinical Trial Eligibility & Patient Matching Engine

Matches patient descriptions to clinical trials. A patient note goes in, and a ranked list of trials the patient may qualify for comes out. This is a research and portfolio project, **not a clinical tool**.

> **Disclaimer:** This project is for research and learning only. It must not be used to make medical or enrolment decisions. Any output is decision support for a human trial coordinator.

## Status

Work in progress. Today's progress:

- [x] Colab environment (T4 GPU) set up and dependencies verified
- [x] Hugging Face access configured (fine-grained token stored as a Colab secret, not in code)
- [x] TREC Clinical Trials 2021 loaded through `ir_datasets` (75 patient queries, 375,580 trials, relevance judgments)
- [x] First data inspection: query text, trial fields, and label distribution per query
- [ ] BM25 baseline and evaluation function (nDCG@10, P@10, recall@100)
- [ ] Dense retrieval baseline (`BAAI/bge-base-en-v1.5`)
- [ ] Biomedical retrieval (MedCPT query and article encoders)
- [ ] Reranking (`bge-reranker-base` vs `MedCPT-Cross-Encoder`)
- [ ] NER fine-tuning (BiomedBERT vs Bio_ClinicalBERT) and NER-based query building
- [ ] Exclusion-criteria handling (stretch goal)

## Problem

Finding patients for clinical trials means reading long clinical notes and long eligibility criteria by hand. This project builds a retrieval and ranking pipeline to shortlist likely trials automatically.

A key difficulty: plain embedding similarity measures topical closeness, not eligibility. "No history of diabetes" and "Exclusion: history of diabetes" score as very similar, so the pipeline needs a way to deal with negation and exclusion criteria.

## Data

| Item | Source |
|---|---|
| Patient queries, trials, relevance judgments | TREC Clinical Trials 2021 via `ir_datasets` (`clinicaltrials/2021/trec-ct-2021`) |

Relevance labels: `2` = eligible, `1` = excluded, `0` = not relevant. Each query has roughly 400 to 550 judged trials.

Trial fields: `doc_id`, `title`, `condition`, `summary`, `detailed_description`, `eligibility`.

No real patient data is used or stored in this repository.

## Planned pipeline

```
Patient note
  -> clinical NER (symptoms, diagnoses, history)
  -> compact patient query
  -> retrieval over trials (BM25, bge, MedCPT)
  -> cross-encoder reranking
  -> (stretch) per-criterion eligibility check
  -> ranked trials
```

## Models

| Role | Models |
|---|---|
| General embedder (baseline) | `BAAI/bge-base-en-v1.5` |
| Medical embedder | `ncbi/MedCPT-Query-Encoder`, `ncbi/MedCPT-Article-Encoder` |
| NER (fine-tune and compare) | `microsoft/BiomedNLP-BiomedBERT-base-uncased-abstract-fulltext`, `emilyalsentzer/Bio_ClinicalBERT` |
| Reranker | `BAAI/bge-reranker-base`, `ncbi/MedCPT-Cross-Encoder` |

## Evaluation

- **Retrieval:** nDCG@10, P@10, recall@100 against the TREC relevance judgments. First pass ranks each query's judged trials, then widens to the full collection.
- **NER:** entity-level precision, recall and F1.
- **Ablations:** raw note vs NER-based query; general vs medical embedder; with and without reranking.

Results will be added here as experiments are run.

## Setup

Run in Google Colab with a T4 GPU.

```bash
pip install sentence-transformers transformers datasets accelerate
pip install rank_bm25 pandas pyarrow scikit-learn requests tqdm
pip install ir_datasets
```

Note: `seqeval` fails to build on Colab's Python 3.13, so NER scoring will use a small hand-written entity-level F1 function.

## Security

Never commit access tokens. Store the Hugging Face token as a Colab secret and read it with `google.colab.userdata`. Use a fine-grained token limited to this project.

## Known limitations

- Embedding similarity alone does not capture negation or numeric eligibility rules.
- MedCPT's query encoder is built for short queries, so raw notes need to be condensed first.
- NER training data (abstracts) differs from clinical notes, so expect some domain shift.
